Rivet provides C++, C, Python, Rust, SwiftPM, and Lua project templates and tasks. Its process and filesystem adapters target macOS and Linux. The plugin requires Neovim 0.11+; installation with `vim.pack` requires Neovim 0.12+. The optional CLI requires Lua 5.1+ or LuaJIT.

## C++ modes

| Choice | Project interfaces | Standard library |
| --- | --- | --- |
| C++17 | Headers and source files | Headers |
| C++20 | Headers, single-file modules, or split modules | Headers |
| C++23 / C++26 | Headers, single-file modules, or split modules | Headers, or opt-in `import std` |

C++26 is the default. Generated targets require the selected standard and disable compiler extensions. Accepting a standard flag does not establish support for every feature of that edition. C++11 and C++14 are not offered.

Named modules require C++20 or later; `import std` requires C++23 or later. These are independent options. A C++17 project defaults to headers. Existing manifests without `module_layout` retain the split layout. See [modules and headers](/docs/modules/) for option defaults and syntax.

## C/C++ toolchain requirements

| Workflow | Requirements enforced by the generated project |
| --- | --- |
| Conventional headers | CMake 3.30+ and a compiler supporting the selected standard; Ninja or Unix Makefiles |
| Named C++ modules | CMake 3.30+, Ninja 1.11+, and compatible compiler dependency scanning |
| `import std` | CMake **4.4.0–4.4.4**, Ninja 1.11+, and a compiler/standard-library combination that advertises the selected C++23 or C++26 import mode |

The `import std` range is Rivet's version-specific experimental gate, not a claim about what other CMake releases can support. Toolchain metadata must belong to the selected compiler and standard library. [Module troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting/#standard-library-modules-are-rejected) covers missing metadata and rejected versions.

Rivet's CMake task adapter requires an explicit build directory inside the project and rejects symlink paths and in-source builds. Multi-config runs require a build preset identifying the configuration. See [CMake reference](/docs/cmake/).

## Automated coverage

The repository's [Checks workflow](https://github.com/guzman109/Rivet/blob/main/.github/workflows/ci.yml) defines macOS and Ubuntu jobs. Both run Lua, LuaJIT, Neovim, and CMake integration suites. CMake is pinned to 4.4.4; macOS installs Homebrew LLVM, while the Ubuntu 26.04 container installs Clang and libc++ 21 development packages.

The [integration suite](https://github.com/guzman109/Rivet/blob/main/tests/integration.lua) exercises headers in C++17/20/23/26, both named-module layouts in C++20/23/26, and selected C++23/26 `import std` combinations. It also covers preset selection, compilation databases, generated-file protection, and executable discovery. These are test definitions; support for a particular checkout and environment depends on its run results.

GCC is not exercised by that CI configuration. Rivet's current module workflow is validated around Clang; GCC modules and `import std` are outside its tested combinations.

Additional checks in [tests/batches.lua](https://github.com/guzman109/Rivet/blob/main/tests/batches.lua) cover C, Python, Cargo, SwiftPM, OpenMP, and optional graphics SDKs. The standard CI workflow does not run that entire suite: its Linux job selects the native Python case. GPU tests require their SDKs and a usable device/context. MPI and CUDA checks cover generation; Vulkan has a header compilation check. These do not establish working MPI, CUDA, or Vulkan runtimes.

## Other providers and workloads

See [C](/docs/c/), [Python](/docs/python/), [Rust](/docs/rust/), [SwiftPM](/docs/swift/), and [Lua](/docs/lua/) for provider-specific tools and tasks. Native package managers own dependencies and lockfiles.

[Graphics starters](/docs/graphics/) need separately installed SDKs. Metal and Xcode workflows require macOS. SwiftUI, signing, simulators, and previews remain Xcode workflows. WebGPU support targets native Dawn applications; browser deployment is not generated.

[HPC hooks](/docs/hpc/) invoke explicit commands for profiling or scheduling. Rivet does not supply a cluster allocator, background job monitor, or remote deployment service.
